Extend the Wi-Fi-only trusted-LAN page (patch 0009) so GET / renders
a scannable Matter QR from the real MT value returned by
GET /api/pairing, and add a new local route GET /qrcode.js that
serves an MIT-licensed QR-generator library gzip-encoded.

Scope, and only this scope:
  * NEW main/aliro_local_web_qrcode_asset.cpp: the pinned gzip-encoded
    installer/vendor/qrcode.js as an extern-linkage byte array. SHA-256
    of the served bytes is dc04ef86fde7887e95d5c22aa468c1e01e0d9ac0101e87f902ee3c93aed0d21a.
    MIT notice + upstream URL preserved in the file header.
  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.cpp: the HTML page now embeds a small
    inline JavaScript that fetches /api/pairing, sets the MT payload
    and manual code via textContent (never innerHTML with untrusted
    JSON), and builds a QR SVG whose numeric coordinates come from
    the qrcode module bitmap alone. A visible #error element shows a
    clear message on pairing-fetch or QR-render failure. The static
    HTML is a compile-time literal with no server-side interpolation.
    GET /qrcode.js serves the gzip asset with Content-Type
    application/javascript and Content-Encoding gzip; the route
    handler is registered as the fourth URI handler (max_uri_handlers
    is bumped from 3 to 4).

This patch is applied only to the Wi-Fi variants (nanoc6-wifi and
atoms3-lite-wifi) via variants.json source_patches; the Thread
variant never applies, compiles, links, or serves any of it.

Explicit non-goals for this patch: no CDN, no remote QR service, no
filesystem, no WebSocket, no settings mutation, no logs endpoint, no
reset / reboot / OTA route. No new dependencies. Preserves every
serialization, cleanup, rollback and reconciliation invariant from
patch 0009 and its corrections.

